From: OpenWrt packaging fix
Subject: csdr.c: fix heap buffer overflow in convert_f_s24/convert_s24_f

Both command handlers allocated s24buffer using the_bufsize *before*
calling initialize_buffers() - but the_bufsize is a global that
starts at 0 (`int the_bufsize = 0;`) and is only ever set inside
initialize_buffers() itself. So s24buffer was allocated as a 0-byte
buffer, then every read/write into it (the_bufsize*3 bytes once
the_bufsize gets its real value) overflowed the heap.

convert_s24_f's overflow goes through fread(), which glibc's
_FORTIFY_SOURCE instruments and catches at runtime:
  *** buffer overflow detected ***: terminated
(reproduced with a plain -O2 build on this distro's default gcc,
which enables _FORTIFY_SOURCE by default).

convert_f_s24's overflow writes through a hand-written byte-by-byte
loop (output[k++]=...), which FORTIFY does not instrument, so it
silently corrupts adjacent heap memory instead of crashing - this
was intermittently corrupting unrelated pipeline data (confirmed via
a round-trip test: convert_f_s24 | convert_s24_f no longer reproduced
the original float stream until this ordering was fixed).

Fix: call initialize_buffers() first (which sets the real
the_bufsize), then allocate s24buffer.

--- a/csdr.c
+++ b/csdr.c
@@ -606,8 +606,8 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
     if(!strcmp(argv[1],"convert_f_s24"))
     {
         int bigendian = (argc>2) && (!strcmp(argv[2],"--bigendian"));
-        unsigned char* s24buffer = (unsigned char*)malloc(sizeof(unsigned char)*the_bufsize*3);
         if(!sendbufsize(initialize_buffers())) return -2;
+        unsigned char* s24buffer = (unsigned char*)malloc(sizeof(unsigned char)*the_bufsize*3);
         for(;;)
         {
             FEOF_CHECK;
@@ -620,8 +620,8 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
     if(!strcmp(argv[1],"convert_s24_f"))
     {
         int bigendian = (argc>2) && (!strcmp(argv[2],"--bigendian"));
-        unsigned char* s24buffer = (unsigned char*)malloc(sizeof(unsigned char)*the_bufsize*3);
         if(!sendbufsize(initialize_buffers())) return -2;
+        unsigned char* s24buffer = (unsigned char*)malloc(sizeof(unsigned char)*the_bufsize*3);
         for(;;)
         {
             FEOF_CHECK;
